      "The rights are not a copy of every button in a foreign privacy law. Access under section 11 is a right to obtain a summary, a picture of processing, and identities of those with whom data was shared, plus other prescribed information, subject to a proviso for certain law-enforcement sharing. Correction and erasure under section 12 have their own limits where retention is necessary. Grievance redressal under section 13 must be tried with the fiduciary or Consent Manager before the Board. Nomination under section 14 is for death or incapacity. Later modules take each right in turn.",
      "Duties sit beside rights. Section 15 expects the Data Principal to comply with applicable law, not to impersonate another person, not to suppress material information for specified State identity documents, not to file a false or frivolous grievance, and to give authentic information when seeking correction or erasure. The Schedule maximum for breach of those duties is Rs 10,000. It is a maximum, and it is not in force until the penalty provisions commence on 13 May 2027.",
      "A Consent Manager, once registration exists, can be a channel for giving and managing consent, and section 13 also speaks of grievance redressal provided by a Data Fiduciary or a Consent Manager. Registration under section 6(9) commences on 13 November 2026, still after the review date. An organisation's own portal is the fiduciary's channel. It is not automatically a Consent Manager. Consent Guru can help an organisation receive requests. It does not become the Board, and it does not create rights before 13 May 2027.",
      "Prepare the paths without pretending they are live statutory remedies. A recommendation is to decide who will read an access request, who can correct a record, and who answers a complaint, and to write the time limit only when the commenced rules prescribe it. Do not invent a number of days. Tell customers the truth: the rights commence on 13 May 2027, and until then a helpful response is customer service, not a claim that section 11 is already enforceable.",
