      "Reasonable security safeguards are the heart of the section's protection duty, together with appropriate technical and organisational measures. Reasonable does not mean a particular brand of software named in the Act, and it does not mean a shrug. A payroll bureau that leaves salary files in an open shared drive is a teaching example of what will be hard to call reasonable once the section is in force. The Schedule maximum for failure to take reasonable security safeguards is up to Rs 250 crore. It is a maximum, imposed by the Board, and the penalty provisions commence on 13 May 2027. Do not tell the bureau the fine has already been assessed.",
      "If a personal data breach happens, section 8 requires intimation to the Board and to each affected Data Principal, in the form and manner prescribed. Memorise 'form and manner prescribed' and forget the imported 72-hour slogan. The Act does not set that hour count. The rules that prescribe the form and manner are in the operational tranche commencing on 13 May 2027. A playbook can still say who to call internally, how to stop the bleeding, and where the contact list of affected people will come from. Label the statutory intimation as commencing later. The Schedule maximum for failure to notify a breach is up to Rs 200 crore, again a maximum, again not yet in force.",
      "Erasure and purpose are obligations, not only customer-service gestures. Unless the law requires retention, erase when consent is withdrawn or as soon as it is reasonable to assume the specified purpose is no longer served, and make the processor erase too. The deeming period is as prescribed. A campaign agency that keeps a political-survey list forever 'just in case,' with no legal retention duty, is the example to challenge. Do not print a fake statutory retention number. Section 8 commences on 13 May 2027. Cleaning a list early can be a recommendation. Calling the clean-up a completed statutory duty on the review date would overclaim.",
      "People need a human to write to. Section 8 requires publication of the business contact information of a Data Protection Officer, if applicable, or of a person who can answer questions about the processing. 'If applicable' matters. The full Data Protection Officer appointment, based in India and answerable to the governing body, is a Significant Data Fiduciary obligation under section 10, and only after the Central Government notifies that status. A neighbourhood clinic is not an SDF by hope or by fear. It still needs a named person. Publish the name as preparation, and do not describe section 8 as already enforced on 2 October 2026.",
      "Grievance redressal under section 13 has to be effective, and section 8 expects the fiduciary to have the mechanism. Put the contact and the path where the notice can point to them. A Consent Manager can also provide a grievance means, but only a registered one, and registration is not in force on the review date. Do not list Consent Guru, or your own banner, as that registered person. List your own officer or contact. For example, a marketplace can name its privacy desk in the seller agreement and in the customer notice, with a footnote that the statutory mechanism commences on 13 May 2027.",
      "Give the leadership team a closing check that does not smuggle in foreign grounds. Are we the fiduciary? Is there a contract path for each processor? Where do we make decisions about people, and is that data accurate? What are our safeguards? Who do we tell, in the prescribed way, if a breach happens after 13 May 2027? What do we delete, and whom can a person contact? None of those questions is answered by a GDPR legitimate-interest paragraph. This module is education. It is not a security audit, and it is not legal advice that your current controls are reasonable.",
